{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~1–4 EUR, larger at longer horizons). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Downtrend underreaction: in falling regimes the model does not drop fast enough and stays anchored to prior higher levels. Increase sensitivity to recent negative momentum (e.g., if 10–20d slope < 0, subtract an extra drift term).",
    "Mean-reversion overestimation: model repeatedly predicts rebounds back toward ~70–75 even when the market continues to grind lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent lows are being made (e.g., if price is below 20d MA and MA is falling, damp rebound forecasts).",
    "Horizon error amplification: errors widen with forecast horizon (days 10–30 especially), with excessive upward curvature. Impose a horizon-dependent shrinkage toward a lower baseline (e.g., progressively pull forecasts toward recent 5–10d average minus a discount).",
    "Spike/uptick overshoot: after short-term upticks (e.g., 71→73), the model extrapolates too much upside; realized path is flatter or resumes decline. Cap upside follow-through after a 1–3d rally unless trend has turned (confirm with MA crossover)."
  ]
}
